# UST.G3CX H11 DM Source-Observation Corridor Theorem

## Status

`CLOSED_EXACT_H11_DM_SOURCE_OBSERVATION_CORRIDOR_AND_248_ZERO_KERNEL_COLUMNS`

## Statement

Let

```text
Q(gamma) = W(gamma) A0(gamma)^-1 U(gamma)
```

be the reduced H11 path action from UST.G3CW. The exact B89 H11 complement
has dimension `4426` and an upper-triangular Dulmage-Mendelsohn condensation
with `1813` components. In that directed graph, only components lying both

```text
downstream of a W-observation component
```

and

```text
upstream of a U-source component
```

can contribute to `Q`.

The exact intersection contains `394` components of total dimension `2786`.
Its block-size multiset is

```text
1710, 684, and 392 copies of 1.
```

Thus the characteristic-zero `Q` action needs precisely the two already known
large H11 cores and 392 scalar blocks. None of the intermediate blocks of
sizes 2 through 49 lies on a source-to-observation path.

## Exact Kernel Zeros

The same directed separation proves that `248` of the `988` columns of `Q`
vanish identically over `Q(gamma)`. Only `740` source columns can reach a
`W` observation. All `988` output rows remain observable.

The G3CW good reduction independently confirms exactly this column support.
At each of the six split roots, the modular rank of `Q` is `710`. Therefore

```text
710 <= rank_Q(gamma)(Q) <= 740.
```

The lower bound follows from good reduction; the upper bound follows from the
exact 740-column source corridor. This theorem deliberately does not promote
the repeated modular value `710` to equality.

## Execution Consequence

For an Archimedean kernel evaluation, only the 740 active columns must be
propagated through the 2786-dimensional corridor. Once a reduced solution `z`
is known, the recovery term `Pz` can be obtained by one combined solve on
`U z`; materializing all 988 columns of `P` is unnecessary. The complete
recovery ancestor graph has dimension `4013`.

This is an exact graph and support reduction. It is not an Archimedean ball
solve, a path integration, an integral marking, or a `beta_C` result.
